Probe for two Part 2 LLM claims about Django migrations (CH01). Windows 11, Python 3.11.9, Django 4.1.13.
Setup: a copy of data/code (original); the example app gets its first migration, like a real project would have.

$ python manage.py makemigrations our_app
Migrations for 'our_app':
  example\our_app\migrations\0001_initial.py
    - Create model OurModel

--- 1) put the REFACTORED models.py (commit c9978f5, CH01 refactoring only) in place
$ python manage.py makemigrations --check --dry-run
No changes detected
exit code: 0

--- 2) put the CH01 FEATURE models.py (commit 8e5cd10, 'txt' added) in place
$ python manage.py makemigrations --check --dry-run
No changes detected
exit code: 0

--- 3) why no change is detected: the saved migration does contain the old validator list ...
3:import django.core.validators
44:                        validators=[
45:                            django.core.validators.FileExtensionValidator(
... but when Django rebuilds the field from the migration, ContentTypeRestrictedFileField.__init__
    replaces the validators it is given with the current list (models.py, 'kwargs["validators"] = ...'):
    field rebuilt with validators [pdf, html] really has: ['pdf', 'html', 'txt']
Result: the CH01 change needs no new migration in projects that use the app.
